            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"God's covenant, mercy, and faithfulness, specifically representing His promise of peace and His pledge not to destroy the earth by flood (the rainbow).\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Genesis 9:13-16\",\"Ezekiel 1:28\",\"Revelation 4:3\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Human military might, strength, and warfare, often used to contrast human power with reliance on God.\",\"basis\":[\"Psalm 44:6\",\"1 Samuel 2:4\",\"Hosea 1:5\",\"Jeremiah 49:35\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Divine judgment and wrath, depicting God as a warrior preparing to strike the wicked.\",\"basis\":[\"Psalm 7:12\",\"Lamentations 2:4\",\"Lamentations 3:12\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Conquest, earthly aggression, false peace, or the Antichrist (associated with the rider on the white horse).\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 6:2\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The Hebrew word 'qesheth' translates to both the meteorological rainbow and the archer's weapon. While the rainbow is the most globally recognized Christian symbol of God's covenant (an 'unstrung' bow pointed toward heaven), the weaponized bow is frequently used in biblical poetry and prophecy to symbolize either human military strength (which is easily broken by God) or impending divine judgment.\"}",
